{"id": 54, "ts": 1779720888.5167391, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "agent_1", "message": "I remain convinced that **Candidate\u202fC** is the strongest overall fit.\u202fWhile we have been weighing B\u2019s conscientiousness and stress handling against C\u2019s egocentric style, the decisive factor is that C is documented as **100\u202f% reliable**, creates a **positive crew atmosphere**, and **makes correct decisions quickly**\u2014covering operational reliability, crew cooperation, and decision quality in one package.\u202fIn contrast, **Candidate\u202fA** still lacks any evidence on the core safety pillars we need: the recent response from Markus confirms we have **no observations of A\u2019s reliability or stress resilience**.\u202fEven though A can **anticipate dangerous situations**, sees **complex connections**, and has **excellent spatial vision** and **very good leadership qualities**, he is also noted as **sometimes not good at taking criticism** and **can be unorganized**, which raises concerns about his consistency and adaptability under long\u2011haul pressures.\u202fGiven the absence of concrete data on A\u2019s reliability and stress handling, and the clear safety\u2011critical strengths already demonstrated by C, I keep my vote for **Candidate\u202fC**."}
